WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:04.997
A tap opens above a bright water tank. The
meter begins to rise, but its

00:00:04.997 --> 00:00:07.606
number describes a flow rate, not the
water collected.

00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:20.695
Freeze the flow for one short minute. A
rectangle appears, with time along its

00:00:20.695 --> 00:00:23.939
base and litres per minute standing along
its height.

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:34.714
Now the flow changes. Four readings build
four steps across the screen.

00:00:34.714 --> 00:00:38.439
Their heights differ, and thin empty
wedges remain above each step.

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.236
Split each minute into smaller pieces. The
staircase becomes finer, and the

00:00:50.236 --> 00:00:54.439
empty gaps shrink. The tank itself has not
changed at all.

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:03.939
The tiny contributions gather into one
shaded shape.

00:01:03.939 --> 00:01:08.872
Its area represents accumulated water,
because a rate multiplied by time gives

00:01:08.872 --> 00:01:10.106
a volume again.

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:18.508
The meter still moves, and the tank still
fills.

00:01:18.508 --> 00:01:21.049
How much water arrives during these four
minutes?

00:01:21.049 --> 00:01:23.272
Find a total from a changing rate.
